<%#
  T-26a increment 3 — one rendered report, in a MY-PAGE block.

  The sibling of `reporter_project_pages/_report.html.erb`, and deliberately not the same
  partial: a my-page block renders in `MyController`'s view context, which sees its own
  helper, `ApplicationHelper` and the five core helpers `MyController` declares — and none
  of ours (`include_all_helpers = false`, HANDOVER §1). So this file may call `l`, core's
  helpers and MODULES, and nothing else.

  That is why `RedmineReporterDashboards::ReportFrame` and `…::DegradationText` are modules
  rather than helper methods: they are the two things both surfaces need, and each has one
  definition that the template editor's helper now delegates to.

  --- WHAT IS DELIBERATELY MISSING COMPARED WITH THE PROJECT DASHBOARD ---

  No PDF export link. `report_pdf_reporter_project_page_path` is project-scoped and my-page
  has no project; inventing a second, project-less export route would be a second way of
  doing something that already has a way, for a button the base plugin's own my-page widget
  never had either.

  No settings/close controls: core's `MyHelper#render_block` supplies those, which is also
  why this partial must never render as nothing — a block with no content loses the very
  controls that would let somebody remove it.
%>
<h3>
  <%= widget.template.name %>

  <% if widget.query %>
    (<%= "#{widget.query.project} |" if widget.query.project %>
    <% options = (widget.query.try(:as_params) || {}) %>
    <% if widget.query.is_a?(IssueQuery) %>
      <%= link_to widget.query.name, _project_issues_path(widget.query.project, options) %>
    <% else %>
      <%= link_to widget.query.name, _time_entries_path(widget.query.project, nil, options) %>
    <% end %>)
  <% end %>

  (<%= widget.outcome.total_count %>)
</h3>

<%# §Findings S-14, and WHICH sentence is honest depends on whether this widget has a
    project (T-52). With none, `state` would answer `:none` for a nil project and print "your
    role does not let you see spent time in this project" over a report drawing on four of
    them, so `state_across_projects` is the honest version. With one, the reverse is true:
    `state_across_projects` answers `:all` as soon as ANY membership grants it, which would
    leave a reader with no notice at all over a project where their role shows only their own
    hours. `time_entry_notice_key` takes the PROJECTS THE ROWS ACTUALLY CAME FROM — which is
    not the same as the project that was chosen, because a saved query widens the bound to
    the subtree — and answers the worst state among them. %>
<% rrd_notice = RedmineReporterDashboards::WidgetReport.time_entry_notice_key(
     widget.template, user, project_ids: widget.project_ids
   ) %>
<% if rrd_notice %>
  <p class="warning"><%= l(rrd_notice) %></p>
<% end %>

<% if widget.outcome.degradations.any? %>
  <div class="reporter-degradations">
    <p><strong><%= l(:label_reporter_preview_degradations) %></strong></p>
    <ul>
      <% widget.outcome.degradations.each do |degradation| %>
        <li><%= RedmineReporterDashboards::DegradationText.for(degradation) %></li>
      <% end %>
    </ul>
  </div>
<% end %>

<% if widget.outcome.ok? %>
  <%# T-44 — THE ASSET GOES IN THE BODY HERE, AND IT HAS TO.
      `content_for :header_tags` is what every other surface uses, and it does nothing from a
      my-page block: this partial renders inside `#content`, long after the layout emitted
      the head. A `script src` in body position is executed where it stands, which is all
      that is needed. It is the SAME file the dashboard and the template pages include, and
      it guards itself against being initialised twice, so three report widgets on one page
      still install one listener. %>
  <%= javascript_include_tag 'redmine_reporter_dashboards', plugin: 'redmine_reporter_dashboards' %>
  <% widget.outcome.sections.each do |section| %>
    <%= RedmineReporterDashboards::ReportFrame.frame(
          section.body, title: l(:label_reporter_report_frame),
          css_class: RedmineReporterDashboards::ReportFrame::WIDGET_CHROME) %>
  <% end %>
<% else %>
  <%# INV-5: the error is never the document. The headline and the correlation id, which is
      the one value FR-58 asks a reader to quote. %>
  <p class="nodata reporter-report-diagnostic">
    <%= l(RedmineReporterDashboards::Reporting::Diagnostic::ORIGIN_LABEL_KEYS
            .fetch(widget.outcome.diagnostic.origin)) %>
    <%= l(:label_reporter_report_diagnostic_correlation_id) %>:
    <code><%= widget.outcome.diagnostic.correlation_id %></code>
  </p>
<% end %>
